Deleting `impl Default for IsolationSide` didn't delete the `#[cfg(feature = "isolation")]` line above it. That attribute now applies to the next item, which is `PatternJavascript`:

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/pattern.rs" lines="64-83"}

In the base commit, `PatternJavascript` had no gate. `crates/tauri/src/manager/webview.rs` imports it and constructs it unconditionally (lines 25 and 131):

::code{file="crates/tauri/src/manager/webview.rs" lines="131-134"}

`isolation` is not a default feature (`default = ["wry", "compression", "common-controls-v6", "dynamic-acl", "x11"]`). So `cargo build -p tauri` with default features fails with an unresolved import of `PatternJavascript`. Ironically, `cargo clippy --all-features` would still pass, which may be why this slipped through.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/pattern.rs" lines="78-83"}
#[derive(Template)]
#[default_template("../scripts/pattern.js")]
pub(crate) struct PatternJavascript {
  pub(crate) pattern: PatternObject,
}

This lint-cleanup PR also reverses the mapping from the `tray_icon` crate's button state:

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="31-38"}

Every `TrayIconEvent::Click` sent to Rust listeners and to the JS `onAction` callback will now report `Down` when the button was released, and `Up` when it was pressed. Apps that follow the documented pattern, acting on `button_state == MouseButtonState::Up` (e.g. toggling a window on left-click release), will fire on press instead, and code that filters on a specific state will see the opposite one.

There's no test for this conversion. The existing `tray_event_json_serialization` test builds the event directly and skips `From`, so CI won't catch it.

::suggestion{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="33-36"}
    match value {
      tray_icon::MouseButtonState::Up => MouseButtonState::Up,
      tray_icon::MouseButtonState::Down => MouseButtonState::Down,
    }

It would also be worth adding a small unit test that checks both arms of this conversion.

`crate::Rect` (re-exported from `tauri-runtime/src/dpi.rs`) is defined with enum fields: `position: dpi::Position` and `size: dpi::Size`. The new constructor assigns a `PhysicalPosition` struct literal and a `PhysicalSize` struct literal to them:

::diff{file="crates/tauri/src/tray/mod.rs" lines="138-154"}

Rust doesn't apply `Into` conversions inside struct literals, so this is a type mismatch when the `tray-icon` feature is enabled. Compare the conversion code just below, which uses `rect.position.into()`.

Even with the types fixed, this is new public API (`pub fn` on a `#[non_exhaustive]` public enum) hidden in a "chore: fix clippy warnings" PR. It has no change file, no callers and no tests. I'd remove it. If a test helper is really wanted, propose it separately, and it can just use `Rect::default()` and `PhysicalPosition::default()`.

